;; waydroid_binder -- let Android's APEX-packaged HALs and system_server talk
;; to each other over binder on a Fedora host.
;;
;; STATUS: VERIFIED 2026-10-08 on vidiot01. An A/B boot pair, host Enforcing
;; throughout, settles what the rest of this file could only diagnose:
;;
;;   BARE   (no module)  sys.boot_completed never set in 240 s.
;;                       init.svc.bootanim running, six services [restarting],
;;                       657 AVCs, every one of them
;;                         denied { call } spc_t -> container_runtime_t
;;                       and `binder: N:N transaction credentials failed` in
;;                       the kernel ring buffer alongside them.
;;
;;   MODULE (this file)  sys.boot_completed = 1 after 18 SECONDS.
;;                       init.svc.bootanim stopped, zygote running, and
;;                       vendor.sensors-default -- which had been crash-looping
;;                       -- running. ZERO binder AVCs after the module was
;;                       loaded, against 852 before it. No further
;;                       `transaction credentials failed` in the kernel log.
;;                       SurfaceFlinger's focused window is
;;                       com.google.android.setupwizard/.user.WelcomeActivity
;;                       and a screencap of it is beside this file as
;;                       a16-boot-vidiot01.png.
;;
;; That makes the module proven AS A WHOLE. It is NOT
;; proven permission-by-permission on vidiot01: that host only ever LOGGED the
;; first denial, `call` in the spc_t -> container_runtime_t direction, which is
;; exactly the behaviour the rest of this file is about -- SELinux stops at the
;; first failure, so the other three were never reachable to be logged. They
;; were proven individually on jeremy-laptop02, each at the cost of a boot, and
;; a green boot here cannot retroactively isolate which of the four it needed.
;; Do not read "vidiot01 only logged one denial" as "three rules are surplus".
;;
;; WHY THIS IS NOT JUST A LOCAL WORKAROUND -- Fedora's own policy has the gap.
;;
;; Fedora 43 (selinux-policy-43.10-1.fc43) ALREADY ships a `waydroid` policy
;; module, loaded at priority 200, and it already thinks about binder. Decompiled
;; from the live system with `semodule -E waydroid` and `/usr/libexec/selinux/hll/pp`:
;;
;;   (allow waydroid_t container_runtime_t (binder (call transfer)))
;;   (allow container_runtime_t waydroid_t (binder (call)))
;;   (allow container_runtime_t self (binder (call set_context_mgr transfer)))
;;   (allow container_runtime_t unconfined_service_t (binder (call)))
;;   (allow container_runtime_t unconfined_t (binder (call transfer)))
;;   (allow unconfined_service_t container_runtime_t (binder (call transfer)))
;;   (allow unconfined_t container_runtime_t (binder (call transfer)))
;;
;; Three source domains are covered -- waydroid_t, unconfined_t,
;; unconfined_service_t -- and spc_t is not one of them. There are no dontaudit
;; rules for the binder class. So this is a genuine hole in the distribution's
;; policy rather than a quirk of one machine, and it is worth proposing upstream
;; to selinux-policy rather than carrying forever as a local module. Note also
;; that waydroid_t, which that module is built around, is NOT the domain Waydroid
;; actually runs in here: under LXC the container is container_runtime_t and the
;; APEX HALs are spc_t, which is likely why the gap went unnoticed.
;;
;; TEST HOST, and how to read the provenance in this file. The ATV track moved
;; from jeremy-laptop02 to vidiot01 on 2026-10-08 (Fedora 43 Workstation --
;; traditional, not ostree -- Waydroid 1.6.3-1.fc43, SELinux Enforcing/targeted,
;; i5-9300H, Intel UHD 630 Gen9.5 plus a nouveau GTX 1050, GNOME on Wayland).
;; The per-permission measurements below were taken on jeremy-laptop02 and are
;; left exactly as recorded there. The A/B pair above is vidiot01's, and it
;; confirms the domain pair independently: `ps -eo label` on vidiot01 shows
;; servicemanager and surfaceflinger in container_runtime_t and 38 processes in
;; spc_t. Host details and pre-flight findings are in docs/64.
;;
;; ONE TOOLING TRAP, measured here. `ausearch -m avc -ts today` reports
;; "<no matches>" on vidiot01 while /var/log/audit/audit.log holds hundreds of
;; these records -- binder denials raised from kernel binder threads carry no
;; SYSCALL context for ausearch to anchor to. Grep the log directly. An
;; ausearch-based check will tell you this module was never needed.
;;
;; WHY THIS EXISTS
;;
;; On jeremy-laptop02 (Fedora Silverblue 44, Waydroid 1.6.3, SELinux Enforcing)
;; the WayDroid-ATV Android 16 image never completes boot:
;;
;;   LineageOS 23.2-20260803-UNOFFICIAL-WayDroidATV-waydroid_x86_64
;;   ro.build.version.sdk = 36
;;
;; sys.boot_completed is never set, init.svc.bootanim runs forever, and six
;; APEX-packaged HAL services crash-loop ~58 times each:
;;
;;   media.swcodec
;;   android.hardware.sensors-service.example
;;   android.hardware.gatekeeper-service.nonsecure
;;   android.hardware.memtrack-service.example
;;   android.hardware.security.authgraph-service.nonsecure
;;   android.hardware.security.secretkeeper-service.nonsecure
;;
;; They die registering with servicemanager:
;;
;;   'Check failed: status == STATUS_OK (status=-129, STATUS_OK=0)'
;;   'Attempted to retrieve value from failed HIDL call:
;;      Status(EX_TRANSACTION_FAILED): FAILED_TRANSACTION'
;;   'Failed to register service ...ISecretkeeper/nonsecure because of UNKNOWN_ERROR.'
;;
;; -129 is EX_TRANSACTION_FAILED. The cause is on the HOST, measured 2026-10-08:
;;
;;   avc: denied { call } for comm="android.hardwar"
;;        scontext=system_u:system_r:spc_t:s0
;;        tcontext=system_u:system_r:container_runtime_t:s0
;;        tclass=binder permissive=0
;;
;; 4936 of them in one 30-minute window. The kernel says the same thing from
;; the other side, 1064 times:
;;
;;   binder: N:N transaction credentials failed
;;
;; That string is binder's security_binder_transaction() LSM hook failing --
;; drivers/android/binder.c -- which on Fedora is selinux_binder_transaction(),
;; and that hook is where tclass=binder `call` is checked. The two messages are
;; one event seen from userspace and from the driver.
;;
;; WHY ANDROID 16 AND NOT THE IMAGES THAT WORK
;;
;; The process labels are split. servicemanager, zygote and surfaceflinger run
;; as container_runtime_t; the six failing services all live under
;; /apex/com.android.hardware.* and run as spc_t. So they must cross a domain
;; boundary to register, and that crossing is what is denied. Images that ship
;; these HALs outside an APEX run them in init's own domain, never cross, and
;; never hit this. This is a host-policy gap that Android 16 is simply the first
;; image here to expose -- it is NOT specific to WayDroid-ATV, and bigtab01 will
;; hit it the moment an APEX-HAL image lands there.
;;
;; CORROBORATED FROM THE IMAGE, 2026-10-08, independently of any host's audit
;; log. /vendor/apex/ in the 20260803 image holds exactly five APEXes:
;;
;;   com.android.hardware.gatekeeper.nonsecure.apex
;;   com.android.hardware.memtrack.apex
;;   com.android.hardware.security.authgraph.apex
;;   com.android.hardware.security.secretkeeper.apex
;;   com.android.hardware.sensors.apex
;;
;; Those map ONE-TO-ONE onto five of the six crash-looping services above; the
;; sixth, media.swcodec, is a system-side APEX. The image also carries
;;
;;   service vendor.sensors-default \
;;     /apex/com.android.hardware.sensors/bin/hw/android.hardware.sensors-service.example
;;
;; So "the failing set is exactly the APEX-packaged HAL set" now rests on two
;; independent observations -- host AVCs on one machine, image contents read on
;; another -- which rules out the six being a coincidence. On its own it would
;; not have made the rule set proven; the completed boot recorded at the top of
;; this file is what does that. See docs/64.
;;
;; WHAT WAS RULED OUT FIRST, so nobody re-walks it
;;
;;   Android's own SELinux   `getenforce` inside the container returns Disabled.
;;                           Not an image-policy problem; there are no Android
;;                           AVCs at all, because there is no Android policy.
;;   binder devices          /dev/binder, /dev/hwbinder, /dev/vndbinder all
;;                           present in the container, host-side via binderfs.
;;   vm.unprivileged_userfaultfd
;;                           Was 0; Android 13+ ART wants 1 and system_server
;;                           warns `userfaultfd: MOVE ioctl seems unsupported`.
;;                           Set to 1 and re-tested: IDENTICAL failure, same six
;;                           services, same counts. A real misconfiguration, and
;;                           not this one. Fix it if you like; it changes nothing.
;;   VINTF                   servicemanager does log `Could not find ... in the
;;                           VINTF manifest` for audio.effect, audio.core and
;;                           power.stats. Those are unrelated optional
;;                           interfaces -- memtrack, which DOES fail here, is
;;                           reported `Found ... in device VINTF manifest`.
;;
;; WHY NOT A PRIVATE TYPE, AS THE OTHER TWO MODULES ARGUE FOR
;;
;; waydroid_backlight.cil and waydroid_appfuse.cil both narrow a grant by
;; inventing a private type. That tool does not apply here. Both ends of this
;; transaction are pre-existing process domains the base policy already defines,
;; and the permission is IPC between them, not access to an object we can
;; relabel. There is nothing to give a new type to. What narrowness is available
;; is taken: two named domains, one class, and only the permissions proven below
;; -- not `semanage permissive -a spc_t`, which was how this was diagnosed and
;; which relaxes EVERY check on a domain that runs all privileged containers.
;;
;; HOW THE PERMISSION SET WAS ESTABLISHED
;;
;; Enforcing shows only the first failure in a chain: a blocked `call` means
;; `transfer` is never attempted and so can never be denied or logged. Running
;; `audit2allow` over the enforcing run yields exactly one rule and a false
;; sense of completeness:
;;
;;   allow spc_t container_runtime_t:binder call;
;;
;; This is the trap docs/42 paid 978 failed boots for. So spc_t was made
;; permissive -- `semanage permissive -a spc_t`, host still globally Enforcing --
;; and the boot re-run on 2026-10-08. Permissive logs what it would have blocked,
;; which is how the rest of the set becomes visible:
;;
;;   {call}     spc_t -> container_runtime_t   binder  perm=1    (now allowed)
;;   {transfer} spc_t -> container_runtime_t   binder  perm=1    <- INVISIBLE before
;;   {call}     container_runtime_t -> spc_t   binder  perm=0  1352x  <- REVERSE
;;
;; All six HAL crash-loops stopped. The boot still failed, one layer further in,
;; and the new abort names the remaining rule exactly:
;;
;;   system_server, 51 aborts:
;;     'Could not connect to declared sensors AIDL HAL after 20 retries'
;;
;; The sensors HAL now starts and registers; system_server, in
;; container_runtime_t, cannot call INTO it in spc_t. Hence the reverse rule.
;; Binder IPC is bidirectional and a reply traverses the hook in the opposite
;; direction, so both directions have to be granted, not just the caller's.

;; Proven: 4936 denials under Enforcing, then observed satisfied at perm=1.
;; `transfer` proven by the permissive run -- it is NOT reachable under
;; Enforcing and will not appear in an audit2allow of a failed boot.
(allow spc_t container_runtime_t (binder (call transfer)))

;; Both proven, and each one cost a boot to find. `call` first: 1352 denials at
;; perm=0 during the permissive run, named by system_server's abort message
;; above. Then `transfer`, which was NOT reachable until `call` was granted --
;; with the two-rule version of this module loaded and the host fully Enforcing,
;; the boot produced exactly one denial, 1276 times:
;;
;;   avc: denied { transfer }
;;        scontext=system_u:system_r:container_runtime_t:s0
;;        tcontext=system_u:system_r:spc_t:s0
;;        tclass=binder permissive=0
;;
;; This is the same shape of trap three times over: each granted permission
;; reveals the next, because SELinux stops at the first failure and the rest of
;; the chain is never attempted. The only reliable way to see one layer is to
;; grant the layer above it. Do NOT widen this to (binder (all)) -- the four
;; permissions below are the complete observed set, and `impersonate`, the
;; fourth binder permission, was never requested by anything in three runs.
(allow container_runtime_t spc_t (binder (call transfer)))

;; NOT INCLUDED, and why -- an artifact, not a dependency:
;;
;;   allow systemd_coredump_t container_runtime_t:process signull;
;;
;; audit2allow proposes this from the same window. It is systemd-coredump
;; signalling the processes that were crash-looping. It is a CONSEQUENCE of the
;; bug, not a requirement of the fix; with nothing aborting there is nothing for
;; coredump to signal. Adding it would grant a permission to paper over crashes
;; that should not be happening.
;;
;; Written in CIL rather than .te for the same reason as the other two modules:
;; semodule compiles CIL directly, so this needs no selinux-policy-devel, which
;; on an rpm-ostree host would cost a layered install and a reboot.
